edulcorator.en:
n. A contrivance used to supply small quantities of
   sweetened liquid, water, etc., to any mixture, or to test tubes, etc.;
   a dropping bottle.

effulge.en:
v. t. To cause to shine with abundance of light; to radiate;
   to beam.
v. i. To shine forth; to beam.

emanant.en:
a. Issuing or flowing forth; emanating; passing forth into an
   act, or making itself apparent by an effect; -- said of mental acts;
   as, an emanant volition.

emblazonry.en:
n. The act or art of an emblazoner; heraldic or ornamental
   decoration, as pictures or figures on shields, standards, etc.;
   emblazonment.

embolismical.en:
a. Pertaining to embolism or intercalation;
   intercalated; as, an embolismic year, i. e., the year in which there is
   intercalation.

emplastration.en:
n. The act or process of grafting by inoculation;
   budding.
n. The application of a plaster or salve.

endosarc.en:
n. The semifluid, granular interior of certain unicellular
   organisms, as the inner layer of sarcode in the amoeba; entoplasm;
   endoplasta.

equitableness.en:
n. The quality of being equitable, just, or impartial;
   as, the equitableness of a judge, a decision, or distribution of
   property.

eulogistical.en:
a. Of or pertaining to eulogy; characterized by eulogy;
   bestowing praise; panegyrical; commendatory; laudatory; as, eulogistic
   speech or discourse.

evilness.en:
n. The condition or quality of being evil; badness;
   viciousness; malignity; vileness; as, evilness of heart; the evilness
   of sin.

exorbitancy.en:
n. A going out of or beyond the usual or due limit;
   hence, enormity; extravagance; gross deviation from rule, right, or
   propriety; as, the exorbitances of the tongue or of deportment;
   exorbitance of demands.

experientialism.en:
n. The doctrine that experience, either that
   ourselves or of others, is the test or criterion of general knowledge;
   -- opposed to intuitionists.

explodent.en:
n. An instrument or agent causing explosion; an exploder;
   also, an explosive.
n. See Explosive, n., 2.

exposures.en:
n. vulnerability to the elements; to the action of heat or cold or wind or rain; 
n. the act of subjecting someone to an influencing experience
n. the disclosure of something secret
